{
  "slug": "05-speculation-benchmark",
  "number": 5,
  "title": "Speculation benchmark: draft-target against n-gram",
  "chapter": 5,
  "unlockAfterSection": "5.5",
  "estimatedHours": 16,
  "needsGpu": true,
  "summary": "Benchmark draft-target and n-gram speculative decoding against a no-speculation baseline on one open model of 8B parameters or fewer, served with vLLM or SGLang, across batch sizes 1, 8, 32 and 128 at temperature 0 and 0.8. The README opens with the decision: which method to turn on, and up to which batch size.",
  "deliverables": [
    "One open target model of 8B parameters or fewer, served with vLLM or SGLang, and a smaller draft model from the same family.",
    "Three configurations on the same engine: baseline, draft-target and n-gram.",
    "The full grid for each configuration: batch 1, 8, 32 and 128 at temperature 0 and 0.8, 24 runs in all.",
    "A warm-up before every run, then P50 and P95 for TTFT and inter-token latency, per-user tokens per second, total throughput and the draft acceptance rate.",
    "Three charts: charts/speedup-vs-batch.png, charts/itl-p50-p95.png and charts/acceptance-rate.png.",
    "A bench package with a --dry-run path that simulates all three configurations with a tiny stand-in model on CPU, and a Makefile whose check target runs it."
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "One engine, one GPU, one prompt set and one output length across all 24 runs. Only the method, batch size and temperature change.",
    "Pick a prompt set that fits the use case you name, and report its mean input and output length.",
    "make check runs only the dry run, finishes in under 8 minutes on 2 CPUs with no network, and writes only under $OUT_DIR.",
    "Report the draft-to-target size ratio and the number of draft tokens per step for each method."
  ],
  "skills": [
    "Speculative decoding",
    "vLLM or SGLang",
    "Benchmark design",
    "Latency percentiles",
    "Technical writing"
  ],
  "rubric": [
    {
      "dimension": "Experimental design",
      "weight": 25,
      "criteria": [
        "All 24 runs are present and share engine, GPU, prompts and output length.",
        "Every run starts after a warm-up, and the README says how long the warm-up was.",
        "The baseline makes each speedup a ratio against the same batch size and temperature."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Measurement",
      "weight": 25,
      "criteria": [
        "results.json reports P50 and P95 for TTFT and inter-token latency in every run.",
        "Acceptance rate is reported for both speculative methods and null for the baseline.",
        "Each run has at least 50 measured requests."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Analysis",
      "weight": 25,
      "criteria": [
        "The README explains why the gain shrinks as batch size grows, in terms of spare compute (see 5.2).",
        "The README explains the temperature effect through acceptance rate (see 5.2).",
        "The README says why n-gram did well or badly on this prompt set (see 5.2.4) and what the draft model cost (see 5.2.1).",
        "TTFT is discussed separately from inter-token latency."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Decision",
      "weight": 15,
      "criteria": [
        "The README opens with a method and a batch-size cutoff, backed by numbers from results.json.",
        "The decision says what a team should do at temperature 0.8."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dimension": "Reproducibility",
      "weight": 10,
      "criteria": [
        "make check runs the same pipeline as the GPU run, with the model swapped for a stand-in.",
        "Launch commands and versions are in the repo."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "requiredFiles": [
    "README.md",
    "results.json",
    "Makefile",
    "charts/speedup-vs-batch.png",
    "charts/itl-p50-p95.png",
    "charts/acceptance-rate.png"
  ],
  "readmeSections": ["## Decision", "## Setup", "## Method", "## Results", "## Limitations"],
